    PF-001 - Payment Follow-up: Default Follow-up Levels
    ====================================================
    Seeds four default account.followup.level records representing the
    standard escalation path for overdue customer invoices, per PF-001
    Scenario 2 "Configure Default Follow-up Levels":

      Level 1 (First Reminder):  sequence=10, delay=7  days, automatic,
                                 friendly tone, no attachments.
      Level 2 (Second Reminder): sequence=20, delay=14 days, automatic,
                                 firmer tone, notifies sales rep.
      Level 3 (Warning):         sequence=30, delay=21 days, automatic,
                                 urgent tone, adds partner to collection
                                 list. PDF attachments OPT-IN (see note
                                 below).
      Level 4 (Final Notice):    sequence=40, delay=30 days, automatic,
                                 final/formal tone, blocks new sales,
                                 adds partner to collection list,
                                 notifies sales rep. PDF attachments
                                 OPT-IN (see note below).

    PF-002 SLA / wkhtmltopdf NOTE — invoice PDF attachments default OFF
    ==================================================================
    Background: Odoo 19's default cron real-time limit (configured via
    the `limit-time-real-cron` config option) falls back to
    `limit-time-real=120` (120 seconds). The PF-002 cron MUST process
    up to ``batch_size=500`` partners within that budget per AAP
    section 0.1.2. Synchronous wkhtmltopdf rendering of one invoice
    PDF per partner with the unpatched-QT wkhtmltopdf binary commonly
    shipped on Linux distributions costs ~1.0 to 1.1 seconds per
    invoice; the QA report measured a 500-partner run with
    attach_invoices=True on Levels 3 and 4 at 556.78 seconds, which is
    4.6x over the default cron timeout. The unpatched-QT binary
    cannot render multiple documents per invocation (it raises
    UserError "Tried to convert multiple documents in wkhtmltopdf
    using unpatched QT"), so the batched render path in
    account_followup_level._batch_render_invoice_attachments cannot
    recover this cost.

    Resolution: ship attach_invoices=False on every default level.
    Operators who want PDF copies attached to follow-up emails can
    opt in per level (the noupdate="1" wrapper preserves their
    customisation across upgrades) after sizing their cron timeout
    accordingly:

      * Patched-QT wkhtmltopdf builds (Odoo's odoofin/wkhtmltox PPA,
        wkhtmltopdf 0.12.5 with the Odoo patches): the batched render
        path engages and 500 partners can fit within the 120 s default.
      * Unpatched wkhtmltopdf (e.g. Ubuntu's ``wkhtmltopdf`` 0.12.6):
        raise the cron real-time limit to ~600 s OR reduce the cron's
        batch size OR keep attach_invoices=False.

    The email body's QWeb t-foreach iteration over the partner's
    overdue invoices already lists every invoice with its number,
    issue date, due date and amount; recipients have all the
    information needed to identify which invoices are overdue without
    PDF attachments. See README.rst section "Performance & SLA"
    for the full operator guide.

    Each level references a mail.template provided by
    data/mail_template_data.xml (external IDs email_template_followup_level_1
    through _level_4). Per __manifest__.py load ordering, mail_template_data.xml
    MUST be listed before followup_data.xml so template external IDs resolve.

    Records are wrapped in <data noupdate="1"> so end-user customisations
    (modifying delays, disabling specific levels, changing action types,
    adjusting minimum amount thresholds) are preserved across module
    upgrades. This mirrors the OCA/Odoo "factory defaults that users may
    modify" pattern, with precedent in:
      - addons/account_bank_reconciliation_ce/data/reconciliation_data.xml
      - addons/account/data/mail_template_data.xml

    Rules Compliance (AAP Section 0.7):
      - R-01 (Module Independence): no refs to sibling new modules
        (account_asset_management, account_budget_management,
        account_deferred_revenue).
      - R-02 (No Enterprise Dependencies): no refs to Enterprise addons
        (account_followup, account_accountant, account_reports).
      - R-03 (N/A): this is data, not model code.
      - R-07 (N/A): this is data, not Python; no sudo() to justify.
      - R-09 (Exact folder name): file resides in
        addons/account_payment_followup/data/.

    SQL Constraint Compliance (models/account_followup_level.py):
      - UNIQUE(sequence, company_id):  sequences 10, 20, 30, 40 are unique.
      - CHECK(delay >= 0):             all delays are non-negative (7, 14, 21, 30).
      - CHECK(min_amount >= 0):        all min_amounts are 0.0.

    action_type value validation:
      The sibling model defines action_type as a Selection field with
      exactly six valid values: 'automatic', 'manual', 'email', 'letter',
      'phone', 'lawyer'. All four default levels use 'automatic' so that
      the PF-002 cron (data/followup_cron.xml) processes them without
      requiring manual intervention. Administrators may switch individual
      levels to 'manual' post-install if human review is desired; the
      noupdate="1" wrapper preserves such customisations.
-->
<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">

        <!--
            ================================================================
            Level 1 of 4 - First Reminder (sequence=10, delay=7 days)
            ================================================================
            Tone: Friendly. Assumes the non-payment is an oversight on the
            customer's part. No side-effect actions are triggered at this
            level; the goal is to re-establish contact and prompt payment
            without damaging the customer relationship.
        -->
        <record id="followup_level_first_reminder" model="account.followup.level">
            <field name="name">First Reminder</field>
            <field name="sequence">10</field>
            <field name="delay">7</field>
            <field name="action_type">automatic</field>
            <field name="email_template_id" ref="email_template_followup_level_1"/>
            <field name="description">First friendly payment reminder sent 7 days past due date. Assumes oversight on the customer's part and thanks the customer for prompt payment. No invoice attachments; no side-effect actions triggered.</field>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
            <field name="attach_invoices" eval="False"/>
            <field name="trigger_block_sales" eval="False"/>
            <field name="trigger_collection_list" eval="False"/>
            <field name="trigger_notify_sales_rep" eval="False"/>
            <field name="min_amount">0.0</field>
        </record>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            Level 2 of 4 - Second Reminder (sequence=20, delay=14 days)
            ================================================================
            Tone: Firmer. Acknowledges the prior contact and requests prompt
            payment. First level at which the sales representative is
            notified so that they can apply relationship pressure in
            parallel with the automated email. Still no sales block, no
            collection list, no invoice attachments.
        -->
        <record id="followup_level_second_reminder" model="account.followup.level">
            <field name="name">Second Reminder</field>
            <field name="sequence">20</field>
            <field name="delay">14</field>
            <field name="action_type">automatic</field>
            <field name="email_template_id" ref="email_template_followup_level_2"/>
            <field name="description">Second payment reminder sent 14 days past due date. Firmer tone requesting prompt payment and referencing the previous reminder. Notifies the assigned salesperson so that relationship-level follow-up can run in parallel with automated email.</field>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
            <field name="attach_invoices" eval="False"/>
            <field name="trigger_block_sales" eval="False"/>
            <field name="trigger_collection_list" eval="False"/>
            <field name="trigger_notify_sales_rep" eval="True"/>
            <field name="min_amount">0.0</field>
        </record>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            Level 3 of 4 - Warning (sequence=30, delay=21 days)
            ================================================================
            Tone: Urgent. Emphasises the seriousness of the overdue status
            and outlines potential consequences. Partner is added to the
            collection list and sales rep is notified. Sales orders are
            NOT yet blocked - collections pressure only.

            attach_invoices defaults to False so that the PF-002 cron
            stays within Odoo 19's default cron real-time limit (120s)
            on the unpatched-QT wkhtmltopdf binary commonly shipped on
            Linux distributions; see the file header for the full
            rationale. Operators who want PDF copies attached can flip
            this flag in Settings > Technical > Followup Levels after
            sizing their cron timeout accordingly. The email body's
            QWeb t-foreach already lists every overdue invoice's number,
            date, due date and amount — recipients have full identifying
            information without the PDF.
        -->
        <record id="followup_level_warning" model="account.followup.level">
            <field name="name">Warning</field>
            <field name="sequence">30</field>
            <field name="delay">21</field>
            <field name="action_type">automatic</field>
            <field name="email_template_id" ref="email_template_followup_level_3"/>
            <field name="description">Formal payment warning sent 21 days past due date. Urgent tone emphasising the seriousness of the overdue status. Adds the partner to the collection list and notifies the assigned salesperson. Sales are not yet blocked at this level. PDF invoice attachments are opt-in per level for SLA reasons (see README.rst Performance section).</field>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
            <field name="attach_invoices" eval="False"/>
            <field name="trigger_block_sales" eval="False"/>
            <field name="trigger_collection_list" eval="True"/>
            <field name="trigger_notify_sales_rep" eval="True"/>
            <field name="min_amount">0.0</field>
        </record>

        <!--
            ================================================================
            Level 4 of 4 - Final Notice (sequence=40, delay=30 days)
            ================================================================
            Tone: Formal / legal. Last automated step before escalation to
            collections or legal action. ALL side-effect actions
            triggered: blocks new sales, adds partner to collection list,
            notifies sales rep. Administrators who prefer human review
            for final notices can switch action_type to 'manual'
            post-install; the noupdate="1" wrapper will preserve the
            customisation across upgrades.

            attach_invoices defaults to False so that the PF-002 cron
            stays within Odoo 19's default cron real-time limit (120s)
            on the unpatched-QT wkhtmltopdf binary commonly shipped on
            Linux distributions; see the file header for the full
            rationale. Operators who want PDF copies attached can flip
            this flag in Settings > Technical > Followup Levels after
            sizing their cron timeout accordingly. The email body's
            QWeb t-foreach already lists every overdue invoice's number,
            date, due date and amount — recipients have full identifying
            information without the PDF.
        -->
        <record id="followup_level_final_notice" model="account.followup.level">
            <field name="name">Final Notice</field>
            <field name="sequence">40</field>
            <field name="delay">30</field>
            <field name="action_type">automatic</field>
            <field name="email_template_id" ref="email_template_followup_level_4"/>
            <field name="description">Final notice sent 30 days past due date before escalation to collections or legal action. Formal tone with clear consequences outlined. Blocks new sales, adds partner to collection list, and notifies the assigned salesperson. PDF invoice attachments are opt-in per level for SLA reasons (see README.rst Performance section).</field>
            <field name="active" eval="True"/>
            <field name="attach_invoices" eval="False"/>
            <field name="trigger_block_sales" eval="True"/>
            <field name="trigger_collection_list" eval="True"/>
            <field name="trigger_notify_sales_rep" eval="True"/>
            <field name="min_amount">0.0</field>
        </record>

    </data>
</odoo>
